            <ArticleTitle>Recurrent Hepatitis B and D Virus Infection in a Liver Transplant Recipient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>175-177</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0041-1345(16)30844-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.transproceed.2016.11.010</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hepatitis B virus (HBV) and hepatitis delta virus (HDV) co-infections progress rapidly and lead to cirrhosis. In Japan, the prevalence of HBV and HDV co-infected patients is low. Therefore, there are few reports of patients with HBV and HDV co-infection having undergone liver transplantation. Herein, we report a rare case of recurrence of HBV and HDV in a 41-year-old man who underwent living donor liver transplantation 4 years prior.</AbstractText>
